The approach reads all of stdin at once, handles each FASTA record separately, and does the work in place on byte arrays.

1. **Read input.** Load stdin with `readAllBytes`. The command-line argument N is ignored because this benchmark is driven entirely by stdin.
2. **Build the complement table.** A 256-entry lookup maps each IUPAC code (upper and lower case) to its uppercase complement: A↔T, C↔G, U→A, R↔Y, K↔M, B↔V, D↔H, and S, W, N to themselves. Any other byte maps to itself.
3. **Process each record.**
   - Copy the header line (starting with `>`) through unchanged.
   - Gather the sequence bytes up to the next `>`, skipping line breaks.
   - Reverse the sequence and complement each base with a two-pointer swap.
4. **Write output.** Emit the result in lines of 60 characters, which is the width the `fasta` generator uses. The output goes into a byte buffer that is written to stdout once.

```java
import java.io.*;

public class revcomp {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {
        byte[] in = System.in.readAllBytes();
        byte[] map = new byte[256];
        for (int i = 0; i < 256; i++) map[i] = (byte) i;
        String from = "ACGTUMRWSYKVHDBN";
        String to   = "TGCAAKYWSRMBDHVN";
        for (int i = 0; i < from.length(); i++) {
            map[from.charAt(i)] = (byte) to.charAt(i);
            map[Character.toLowerCase(from.charAt(i))] = (byte) to.charAt(i);
        }
        int n = in.length;
        ByteArrayOutputStream outBuf = new ByteArrayOutputStream(n + 1024);
        byte[] seq = new byte[n];
        byte[] line = new byte[61];
        int p = 0;
        while (p < n) {
            if (in[p] != '>') { p++; continue; }
            int hs = p;
            while (p < n && in[p] != '\n') p++;
            outBuf.write(in, hs, p - hs);
            outBuf.write('\n');
            if (p < n) p++;
            int len = 0;
            while (p < n && in[p] != '>') {
                byte b = in[p++];
                if (b != '\n' && b != '\r') seq[len++] = b;
            }
            int i = 0, j = len - 1;
            while (i < j) {
                byte a = seq[i], c = seq[j];
                seq[i] = map[c & 0xff];
                seq[j] = map[a & 0xff];
                i++; j--;
            }
            if (i == j) seq[i] = map[seq[i] & 0xff];
            for (int k = 0; k < len; k += 60) {
                int m = Math.min(60, len - k);
                outBuf.write(seq, k, m);
                outBuf.write('\n');
            }
        }
        System.out.write(outBuf.toByteArray());
        System.out.flush();
    }
}
